

MED‑ESE v1.0 — Architectural README

Independent Design by C. Matthew Blackwell

1. Overview

MED‑ESE v1.0 is a three‑layer endogenous medical‑simulation architecture that ingests encrypted clinical data, maps internal physiological state machines, and maintains a verified internal transition ledger. The system is designed for secure analysis, state‑driven modeling, and identity‑stable physiological tracking.

This README provides a structured explanation of each subsystem, its purpose, and its operational flow.

2. Layer I — Secure Clinical Dataset Ingestion & Encryption

Purpose

To convert raw clinical datasets into zero‑knowledge encrypted substrates suitable for homomorphic computation.

Components

EHR Intake — structured medical records

DICOM Intake — imaging data

Genomic Intake — sequence‑level biological data

Client‑Side Zero‑Knowledge Encryptor

Homomorphic Cipher Substrate (CKKS/BFV)

Function

All data is encrypted before entering the system. No plaintext ever touches the internal engines. Homomorphic encryption allows computation on encrypted values without decryption.

